W: 2. The weaver being away from home,
W: Away from home a-drinking,
W: The major he come creeping in
W: With his gay gold guineas jingling.
W:
W: CHORUS:
W: Lie low, Lincoln, low, lady,
W: Low, Lincoln lee.
W:
W: 3. There come a rambling at the door,
W: She said, it's a bold adventure,
W: So under the bed you must go
W: And let my husband enter.
W: CHORUS
W:
W: 4. O dear, O dear, O dear, said she
W: When she found the major missing,
W: I've rolled the bed from side to side
W: For the want of a little kissing.
W: CHORUS
W:
W: 5. O dear, O dear, O dear, said she,
W: There's something else would please me
W: And that's a bottle of good ale,
W: Now, won't you fetch it for me?
W: CHORUS
W:
W: 6. The weaver he jumped out of bed
W: Just like a rambling rake.
W: And the major's britches he put on
W: And slyly crept away.
W: CHORUS
W:
W: 7. He rode, he rode, he rode to town,
W: He found that he had plenty,
W: He found he had a gay gold watch,
W: And guineas more than twenty.
W: CHORUS
W:
W: 8. He rode, he rode, he rode back home
W: He found that he had plenty,
W: He found he'd loons and loons and double loons
W: And gulneas more than twenty.
W: CHORUS
W:
W: 9. O dear, O dear, O dear, said she,
W: Those britches don't become you,
W: They look so neat and so complete
W: I'd like to have them from you.
W: CHORUS
W:
W: 10. O dear, O dear, O dear, said he,
W: I'll lay you down a wager,
W: These britches look as good on me
W: As you do on the major.
W: CHORUS
